      "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\le {{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$\n{{field-3}},\nwe have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\le {{field-5}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}\\le {{field-7}}\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n(1+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{1+1}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(k+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{k+1}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((k+1)+2)^{2} & \\le {3}^{(k+1)+1} \\\\ (k+3)^{2} & \\le {3}^{k+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives:\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+3)^{2}\\le {3}^{k+2}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$",
